{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>In His image.</b> The concept of צלם (image) is an intellectual apprehension, and the intent of saying בצלמנו ('in our image') is that the rational soul in man is drawn from the spirit of His holiness. And there is a great difference between צלם (image) and תאר (form), not according to the opinion of Rabbi David Kimchi, who thought they are one and the same; and so too is the opinion of the Rambam, for there is a great difference between them. As he said at the beginning of his book the Guide: many understood the word צלם to denote, in the holy tongue, the external sensible form, and they erred in this to the point of inclining toward heresy by believing in corporeality. But צלם and תאר are two distinct things: for תאר is said of the external sensible form, in the manner of what is written (I Samuel 28): מה תארו ('what is his form?'), and (Judges 8): כתאר בני המלך ('like the form of the king's sons'). But צלם applies to the natural form, that is, the concept through which the thing is actualized and constitutes its essence, and it is upon this intellectual apprehension that בצלם אלהים ('in the image of Elohim') is said. Therefore it is said (Psalms 73:20): צלמם תבזה ('you will despise their image') — for contempt attaches to the soul, not to the configuration of the limbs. And what is intended here by בצלמו ('in His image') is the species-form, which is the intellectual apprehension, as he further explained in the Book of Knowledge, chapter four." "<b>And there is</b> one who interpreted בצלמנו ('in our image') as 'in the image of ours that is separate from Him' — that is, the image of the world in its structure: the world of the angels, the world of the spheres, and this lower world; and these three parts comprise all of existence. And upon this structure, the structure of the human body is built upon three parts, in likeness to the structure of existence. Because of this, man is called a microcosm, for he corresponds to the great world. And this is what Job hinted at in saying (Job 19:26): ומבשרי אחזה אלוה — 'and from my flesh I shall perceive El' — meaning that through the parts of his body, which are three, he will perceive the three parts of existence in the work of Creation, which are the work of Elohim. For the head in man, where the intellect overflows, corresponds to the supernal world, where the separate intellects reside. From the neck to the loins is a middle part in man, and from there extend two branches — the right hand and the left hand — and in them are included seven measures: the finger, the hand, the palm, the handbreadth, the pinky, the cubit, and the arm — corresponding to the middle world, which is the world of the spheres, which include the seven moving spheres in which the seven wandering stars are fixed. And in this middle part is included the heart in man, from which thought arises; and it is like a king sitting in chambers within chambers, and all the other organs of the body are under its dominion; and it is in the middle of the body, corresponding to the sphere of the sun, which is in the middle of the spheres, from which illumination comes to this lower world, and through whose causation all its actions are performed. From his loins downward is the third part, and in this part is included the function of generation, coming-to-be and passing-away — corresponding to this world, which is subject to coming-to-be and passing-away. Now that this has been explained to you, wherever you find in the Torah mention of corporeal things attributed to the Creator — such as 'the hand of YHWH,' 'the eyes of YHWH,' 'the ears of YHWH,' 'the face of YHWH' — in all of them you can observe that the matter is not literal, but they are words indicating hidden concepts by way of one or another type of wisdom. And this is the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory: 'The Torah always speaks in the language of human beings.'" "<b>And the explanation</b> of this matter: since we are possessors of senses and sense-impressions, we have no power to apprehend intellectual realities except through the representations of sensible things in which we are immersed. Because of this, the Torah's intention was to write its words and matters according to the capacity of the listener's understanding, so that the concept would settle upon his heart at first by way of corporeality, through corporeal terms — and afterward he would grow wise and examine carefully to understand that all this is only by way of approximation and metaphor. And the discerning among those who seek HaShem, blessed be He, will strip away the husks of those words and their corporeality and understand the inner meaning contained within them, that they are words spoken in wisdom. And of this the verse says (Proverbs 28:5): ומבקשי ה׳ יבינו כל — 'and those who seek YHWH shall understand all.' And had the Torah not narrated in corporeal terms close to the intellect of the multitudes, but only in intellectual terms and concepts, the multitudes would have understood neither the words nor the concepts. Because of this, the Book was given in a manner that benefits the wise and the fool alike, and every man perceives in it according to his capacity, wisdom, and level: it benefits the fool in that the concept settles in his heart and mind that there is a Creator who exists, whom he is obligated to serve; and it benefits the wise man in that he understands those words from the perspective of wisdom. And through this, all are equal in the knowledge of the existence of the Creator, blessed be He. And the philosophers of inquiry already drew a parable for this matter: it is like a wealthy man to whom a guest came to lodge — he and his animals — and he gave him all kinds of delicacies to eat, and gave his animals straw and fodder; to each he gave what was fitting for it. So too the Torah expands upon the attributes of the Creator in corporeal metaphors according to the weakness of the multitudes' understanding, and alludes to intellectual concepts for the possessors of wisdom and ethic." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: HaShem deliberated in His seven attributes when making man, and the Midrash of Bereshit Rabbah indicates this — as they said: 'נעשה אדם — in what did He deliberate? Rabbi Yehoshua of Sikhnin says: He deliberated in the souls of the righteous. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi says: He deliberated in the works of heaven and earth.' A parable: to a king who had a councillor and did nothing without his knowledge. Rabbi Ammi said: He deliberated in His heart. A parable: to a king who built a palace through an architect, and it did not please him — upon whom could he vent his anger? Not upon the architect — as it is written: ויתעצב אל לבו ('and He was grieved to His heart'). Rabbi Assi said: a parable — to a king who made merchandise through a broker, and did not profit from it; upon whom could he vent his anger? Not upon the broker — end of quotation." "<b>And there is</b> in this verse a deep concept, sealed in the treasuries of the kabbalists, the seekers of HaShem, for it is the secret of the structure of the ten Sefirot, of which man is composed, and whose form is a likeness to them. בצלמנו — 'in the image made for us'; כדמותנו — 'in our likeness,' the likeness of Tiferet, and it is the extension of the body. And now hear the interpretation of the verse, and everyone who unifies a complete unification should direct his heart to this: the head of man, which is the glory of the entire body, corresponds to Keter, which is the head of all the Sefirot; and it is written (Psalms 119:160): ראש דברך אמת — 'the head of Your word is truth'; and it is written (Song of Songs 5:11): ראשו כתם פז — 'his head is pure gold.' His brain and his palate correspond to Ḥokhmah, which is inward, and it is written (ibid.): חכו ממתקים — 'his palate is sweetness'; and likewise in the word חכמה (Ḥokhmah) is hinted מוח חיך (brain, palate). His tongue corresponds to Binah, which brings forth all the powers into light, as it is written: וכלו מחמדים — 'and all of him is desirable' — so too the tongue brings forth and reveals the powers of thought. And you already know from the Kabbalah that Keter and Ḥokhmah and Teshuvah are all one thing, and so too in the formation of man: the brain and the palate and the tongue, in the organ of the head, are all one thing. His arms, right and left, correspond to Ḥokhmah — and the covenant of the tongue arbitrates between them, for Teshuvah corresponds to the tongue; thus you have the upper Sefirot. Tiferet corresponds in man to the extension of the body, and so too Tiferet is the strength and power of all the Sefirot; and concerning Tiferet, Moses our master, peace be upon him, said (Numbers 16:22): אל אלהי הרוחות לכל בשר. The legs in man, the right leg and the left leg, correspond to Neẓaḥ and Hod. The covenant of circumcision in man corresponds to Yesod, which is called Ẓaddik, which is called Covenant, aligned in the middle — and this is: the covenant of circumcision arbitrates between them. From here you will understand that it is fitting that the time of Torah scholars' intimacy be on the Sabbath, because Yesod is the seventh, when you make the three uppermost into one. And when He saw, after the completion of the body was complete, that Malkhut remained cut off, He said: perhaps, HaShem forbid, there are two authorities — and He did not take to heart that it was the heart, and concerning it He said: ויתעצב אל לבו. And concerning it, the masters of truth brought the forceful parable of the broker and the architect. And it is the etrog, of which our Rabbis of blessed memory said that it resembles the heart; and it stands on its own, not bound together with the lulav, but it is indispensable for the species of the lulav, for the mitzvah is not complete without it — and the discerning will understand. And when a man grasps this great concept, his eyes will be illumined and he will be able to distance from the Torah and the faith every blemish and every calumny, and everything will be as clear as the sun before him, that there is there no likeness or form whatsoever. And so the Torah testified at the holy assembly (Deuteronomy 4:15): כי לא ראיתם כל תמונה — 'for you saw no manner of form'; and it is written (Deuteronomy 4:12): ותמונה אינכם רואים — 'and you see no form.' But these things are said of necessity, and all is a likeness, for the lowest, ultimate formation is a likeness of the supernal Emanation."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:28:23.407146320Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}